    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d450-a1">

                <pb facs="1100" n="1052" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v06p1/pg1052"/>

                <head>Enclosure 1</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret and
                            private</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-07-27">July 27, 1954</date>.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#italic">Prime Minister Churchill to Foreign
                        Minister Molotov</hi></p>
                <p>I am sorry not to have been able to reply before now to your prompt and agreeable
                    message of July 5,<anchor n="4" xml:id="d450-f1"/> but I am sure that from your
                    talks with Mr. Eden you will have realised that I had to wait until the end of
                    the Geneva Conference. I am glad that an Agreement has been reached there and
                    hope that it will not be disturbed.</p>
                <p>After discussion with my colleagues I was about to send you a further message to
                    suggest a meeting say at the end of August or in the first half of September at
                    some half-way house such as Berne, Stockholm or Vienna. But in the meanwhile
                    your Note of July 24 <pb facs="1101" n="1053" xml:id="pg1053"/> in reply to ours
                    of May 7 has been published.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d450-f2"/> This of course does
                    not fit in with the plan I had in mind. My aim and hope was to bring about an
                    informal Two-Power Talk between the Heads of our two Governments, but now after
                    the Geneva Conference ended you have decided to propose a formal Conference of
                    European States and of the United States to discuss again the proposals made
                    some months ago by the Soviet Government on collective security in Europe, which
                    I presume the Heads of Governments would not be expected to attend.</p>
                <p>This has obviously superseded for the time being the small informal meeting I had
                    suggested which might perhaps have been the prelude to a Three- or Four-Power
                    Meeting on the top level.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
